{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Domain 2: Operational reliability","context":"Agents aren't traditional software. Their behavior is shaped by prompts, model versions, context windows, and tool integrations, any of which can shift over time.\n\nThis domain covers two ownership patterns. If you build custom agents on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, then you own the full operational stack, including versioning, evaluation, model upgrades, and incident response. \n\nIf you use AWS DevOps Agent, then AWS provides management of the underlying service and infrastructure, but you still own the configuration, runbooks, and integrations.\n\nThe following sections cover how to build and upgrade agents, plan for load and failure, trace multi-agent workflows, and respond when agents fail","url":"https://repost.aws/articles/ARRrnN9FMVRve7wI2svOTiRg/domain-2-operational-reliability"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AGENTSUS02-BP04 Measure and optimize the environmental footprint of agent workloads","context":"# AGENTSUS02-BP04 Measure and optimize the environmental footprint of agent workloads\n\nWithout measurement, sustainability claims are aspirational and\noptimizations are not tracked against real-world data. Tracking the\nenvironmental footprint of agent workloads makes sustainability an\nengineering metric. Baselines show where effort is worth investing,\nand trends show whether changes are actually working.\n\n**Desired outcome:**\n\n* You have carbon emissions baselines for agent infrastructure\n established across a defined observation period.\n* Resource efficiency metrics for each task (tokens per successful\n completion, compute hours per workflow, and cache hit rates) are\n tracked alongside business outcomes.\n* Deferrable workloads are scheduled during off-peak periods or\n routed to Regions with favorable energy profiles.\n* Operational and sustainability metrics are combined in\n dashboards that inform periodic optimization reviews.\n\n**Common anti-patterns:**\n\n* Claiming sustainability benefits from agent optimizations\n without establishing baselines, making it impossible to validate\n whether changes actually reduced impact.\n* Treating every workload as equally time-sensitive, running batch\n processing and background tasks during peak hours when deferring\n them would reduce contention and energy consumption.\n* Ignoring regional differences in energy infrastructure when\n selecting deployment Regions for workloads with flexible latency\n requirements.\n* Tracking only infrastructure utilization without tying it to\n business outcomes, so efficiency gains in compute don't connect\n to value delivered.\n\n**Benefits of establishing this best\npractice:**\n\n* Measurable baselines make sustainability improvements verifiable\n instead of aspirational.\n* Optimization effort flows to the workloads with the largest\n environmental impact, rather than being applied uniformly.\n* Deferrable workloads run when infrastructure is underutilized,\n improving fleet-wide efficiency without affecting user-facing\n performance.\n\n**Level of risk exposed if this best practice\nis not established:** Medium","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/agentic-ai-lens/agentsus02-bp04.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Audit trails for autonomous agents with AWS DevOps Agent | AWS DevOps & Developer Productivity Blog","context":"### Recommendations polling\n\n**Recommendations** **(API)** are cross-incident preventative advice. The agent generates these on a schedule through a goal, and each recommendation carries a status and a version. Each evaluation run writes new records rather than updating the previous run’s, so advice that persists week over week appears as a series of records. The superseded ones remain at whatever status they last held. “The agent recommended X, the same failure recurred Y weeks later, and here is every version of that advice in between” is something you reconstruct from the archived snapshots, because the API returns current and superseded records together. Recommendations have no Amazon EventBridge event. You capture them by polling on a schedule.\n\n```\naws devops-agent list-recommendations --agent-space-id <id>\n```","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/devops/audit-trails-for-autonomous-agents-with-aws-devops-agent/"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS DevOps Agent adds release management capability (preview) - AWS","context":"AWS DevOps Agent now offers a release management capability in preview, which includes code change reviews for production safety and autonomous release testing. The release readiness review feature evaluates code changes for drift from internal standards, dependency impacts, and access controls, while release testing generates and runs test plans for web and API-based applications to catch regressions, UX issues, and integration failures. To get started, connect your code repositories and pipelines in your AWS DevOps Agent space. The preview is available in the US East (N. Virginia) Region at no additional cost. AWS DevOps Agent release management aims to accelerate and validate deployment, keep applications running optimally, and help teams ship faster with reduced mean time to recovery (MTTR). The production operations features, which are generally available, are listed on the AWS DevOps Agent pricing page","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/aws-devops-agent-release-management/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Introducing AWS DevOps Agent (preview), frontier agent for operational excellence - AWS","context":"AWS DevOps Agent, a new preview service from Amazon Web Services (AWS), is introduced as a frontier agent designed to improve operational excellence in applications running in AWS, multicloud, and hybrid environments. The agent proactively identifies and resolves incidents, reducing Mean Time to Resolution (MTTR), and provides recommendations for observability, infrastructure optimization, and deployment pipeline enhancement. It autonomously triages incidents, analyzing telemetry, code, and deployment data, and works with observability tools, runbooks, code repositories, and CI/CD pipelines. The agent is available at no additional cost during preview in the US East (N. Virginia) Region","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/12/devops-agent-preview-frontier-agent-operational-excellence/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-iam","skill_description":"Provides verified corrections for IAM behaviors that AI agents frequently get wrong — policy evaluation edge cases, trust policy gotchas, STS session limits, Organizations quirks, and SAML/MFA specifics. Also provides structured workflows for IAM role management and baseline policy generation from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON. Covers condition operator safety (ForAnyValue/ForAllValues with Null checks), bucket policy deny patterns (VPC endpoint restrictions, org paths), confused deputy protection, and service role creation for AWS services (Glue, CloudTrail, Lambda, ECS, etc.) with aws:SourceAccount/aws:SourceArn trust conditions. Applies when creating IAM roles, writing IAM or bucket policies, generating policies from application source code or a Terraform plan JSON, working with STS, Organizations, or condition operators, or any task needing a service or execution role. Does not cover non-IAM authorization like Cognito user-pool policies or app-level RBAC.\n\n\nServices: iam, sts, organizations\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure, policy-generation, permissions, source-code-to-policy, terraform-plan-to-policy\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, security-engineer\nWorkload: serverless, containers, data-analytics, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-iam"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-investigation","skill_description":"Investigates what is happening in an AWS account from evidence already recorded there. Not for capturing new data from running code (snapshots of function arguments on a live service, Dynamic Instrumentation), questions answered from the CloudWatch Omni context graph or Application Signals service map, or building or changing infrastructure. Covers reading deployment records instead of the newest stored version (completed AppConfig deployments, the version an alias points to, a stack's current template), following a request through every service's own logs (exact per-layer fields, unique failed requests in an alarm's breach window, failure boundary per event), and answering questions from the account's data (exact counts and breakdowns, coverage of the requested range, exact filters and joins, the rule behind failed or unmatched records, verbatim values). Use to triage an alarm, error spike or failed request, trace a request across services, or count, break down, extract or join records.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, appconfig, lambda, cloudformation, dynamodb, s3, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, investigate, query, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability, operations","skill_name":"aws-investigation"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-transform","skill_description":"Performs code upgrades, migrations, and transformations using the AWS Transform (ATX) CLI. Use when upgrading language versions, migrating AWS SDKs, migrating frameworks (Angular, Vue.js, Spring Boot, React), upgrading libraries, optimizing performance, migrating x86 to Graviton, analyzing codebases / generating documentation, or defining custom transformations with natural language. Runs locally on a few repositories or at scale across hundreds via AWS Batch/Fargate.\n\nServices: transform\nTasks: migrate, upgrade, refactor, analyze\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: ai, migration, modernization\n*","skill_name":"aws-transform"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Checking AWS DevOps Agent docs for sustainability review capability details"}
